2026-09-17 | repo state (git) | The local working copy was a stale ancestor of origin/main — 0 commits ahead, 34 behind. Grading it would have validated code from 2026-09-13 and missed the whole offline-install/model-pinning body of work. The "CI failing on all jobs as of 2026-09-14" premise is stale: upstream fixed it (run 35039888506 on main = success, 2026-09-16). | Preserved the uncommitted ingest-refactor work as branch wip/ingest-refactor-hints (commit 317d2f4) so nothing was lost, then fast-forwarded local main a320ab2 -> 96f5081. | pytest before (stale tree): 870 passed. After alignment: 885 passed, 0 failed, 0 errors.

2026-09-17 | scripts/manage_install.py | update() drops options when it falls back to a full install. On a machine with no .venv, `manage_install.py update --offline-bundle <dir>` called install(pytorch_runtime=...) WITHOUT the bundle, so an offline target would silently attempt networked installs despite being handed local wheels and models. | Forwarded offline_bundle in the fallback call (`return install(pytorch_runtime=..., offline_bundle=offline_bundle)`). Added regression test test_update_forwards_offline_bundle_to_full_install, which failed first with forwarded kwargs {'pytorch_runtime': 'auto'}. | tests/test_manage_install.py: 13 passed.

2026-09-17 | scripts/build_offline_bundle.py | The generated install.bat ran `--offline-bundle "%BUNDLE_DIR%"` where BUNDLE_DIR comes from %~dp0, which always ends in a backslash. The argument therefore expanded to `"C:\...\bundle\"`; PowerShell's argument parser reads the trailing backslash-quote as an escaped quote, mangling the path and breaking the Windows offline install. | Stripped the trailing backslash (`if "%BUNDLE_DIR:~-1%"=="\" set "BUNDLE_DIR=%BUNDLE_DIR:~0,-1%"`) and added the separator explicitly at the repo.tar.gz use site. Pinned the strip and the exact argument form in test_bundle_launchers_invoke_offline_install. | tests/test_offline_bundle.py: 15 passed.

2026-09-17 | scripts/ install + doctor (validation) | Goal 2: validate the install/bootstrap/doctor path end to end without changing machine state. | Validated: `manage_install.py doctor --skip-model-init --ci` and `./doctor_oracle_tts.sh --skip-model-init --ci` both exit 0 (only WARNs, no blocking FAIL); all five .sh wrappers (bootstrap/install/doctor/run/uninstall) resolve Python 3.12, locate the repo root, and dispatch to the correct manage_install subcommand (verified via --help, which exercises the whole wrapper path with no install side effects); every .ps1 wrapper forwards @args; `--offline-bundle` rejects a missing/invalid bundle and a bundle with no wheels for the platform with clear messages; build_offline_bundle.py validated with --skip-models --skip-wheels (exit 0, emits the 4 pinned SHAs into manifest.json and both launchers). A full `bootstrap` was deliberately NOT run: it pip-installs into .venv and writes the launcher to ~/.local/bin (outside the repo) — both out of scope for this pass. | pytest: 885 passed (full suite, see final entry).

2026-09-17 | Noticed, not actioned | `~/.local/bin/the-oracle` is a stale managed launcher pointing at /home/cody/The_Oracle_TTS (an old checkout location), so the doctor's entrypoint check reports WARN. This is a real problem on this machine but the file lives outside the repo, so it was left alone per the "do not change state outside the repo" rule; running ./bootstrap_oracle_tts.sh rewrites it to the current path. Also noted: `update(skip_doctor=True)` cannot forward skip_doctor to install() (install() has no such parameter), so a no-venv update always runs the doctor; cosmetic, left as-is. The doctor's "fallback=" count differs on a fresh tree (0) versus after its own smoke step populates build/real_engine_smoke/inputs (2) — informational, the check PASSes either way.
